WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
 - Hello, and welcome to our final chapter,

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.320
 Retrieving and Visualizing Data.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.800
 In this chapter, we are going to basically

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.100
 bring this all together, databases, web services,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.260
 code, loops, logic, and we're going to solve a problem

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.900
 that is a multi-step data analysis.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.500
 We're going to find some data on the internet,

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.620
 might be HTML, might be an API or whatever,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.960
 and we're going to write a relatively slow process

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.160
 that's going to pull data slowly,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.820
 because these are all rate-limited.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.280
 This is a slow and restartable process.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.060
 So you can be a start this, and what we're going to do

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.220
 is we're going to have a database that's going to hold

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.020
 the data that we're pulling, and so this might take

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.520
 several days, actually, if you really have to do it,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.560
 and then you'll build up your data in your database,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.540
 and then what you tend to do is you tend to produce

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.460
 two databases, one is kind of a raw database that,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.640
 you know, you really, all of its data columns are aimed at

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.420
 helping you figure out what you've got to retrieve yet,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.780
 and what you haven't retrieved yet,

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.220
 so that's kind of a crawling, spidering process,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.660
 and then you find that the data is kind of nasty and ugly,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.660
 and you find that before you're going to do any analysis,

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.580
 you probably want to clean and process it,

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.380
 so in a lot of these, you're going to go from a raw database

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.060
 to a clean one, and this is going to be really large,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.200
 and this is going to be really small,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.320
 and you're going to do this sort of once, but slowly,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.220
 and you'll do this as many times as you need,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.760
 changing this program, cleaning the data up

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.020
 over and over and over again, and then you'll end up

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.480
 with really clean data, and it's relatively small,

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.500
 and you might run programs that'll loop through this

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.860
 to do visualizations or analysis or some things,

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.400
 or whatever, and so you'll actually sort of use this

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.900
 database as a source of information, okay?

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.340
 So that's the basic pattern of what we're going to work with.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.020
 Now, this is what I call personal data mining,

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.860
 and if you're going to do this seriously,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.780
 Python is used in lots of data mining activities,

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.280
 but if you're going to do data mining seriously

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.940
 with really, really large data sets,

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.700
 we're doing small to medium-sized data sets,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.660
 as you might do sort of for individual personal research

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.360
 versus like an organizational research

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.900
 where you're processing the logs of a web server

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.360
 or something like that, and there's lots and lots

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.440
 of wonderful technology, and what's really cool is

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.380
 this technology just keeps getting better and better,

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.420
 because the whole data and mining data analysis,

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.200
 natural language processing field is just so hot right now.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.240
 It's so awesome.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.520
 We're going to keep it simple and do stuff for ourselves

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.740
 for now, and I gave you a bunch of sample code

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.500
 that's going to make it so that you can adapt

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.360
 this sample code to solve the problems

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.060
 that you need to solve.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.800
 So like I said, this is more of a programming exercise.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.540
 Data mining might be a lot more complex.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.240
 If you're doing simple research,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.500
 this might actually model what you do pretty well.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.040
 So the first thing that we're going to do

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.200
 is what's called use the Google's JSON API for geocoding,

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.200
 and there are two versions of this.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.040
 One version requires a key,

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.200
 and one version doesn't require a key.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.620
 Google used to make all this data available for free

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.440
 but with just a rate limit, but now they're making,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.280
 increasingly requiring a key.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.980
 So I give you code in this zip file that kind of does both.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.760
 If you really wanted to do something in production

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.100
 taking user entered places and names

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.320
 and getting precise latitude longitude coordinates

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.480
 so you can produce a nice little Google map like this.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.660
 But since Google has made a rate limited API,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.100
 I've actually pre-spidered a copy of the Google data

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.120
 and I have my own sort of fake Google API

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.460
 and so you can do your assignments

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.220
 and test all your code using my fake API

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.640
 which has no rate limits and has no problems,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.120
 but it's only a limited set of the data.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.700
 And so this is the basic process

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.120
 and it's one of those things that it follows

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.520
 that basic personal data modeling,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.460
 personal data mining pattern.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.100
 And so here's this API which is either Google or me.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.920
 I've got my own Dr. Chuck version of this,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.800
 Dr. Chuck.net version of this.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.800
 And there is an input queue of the location.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.500
 So this is the user data where they just put in the name

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.740
 of where they think they live,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.600
 University of Tubigin or something,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.180
 and so this is the queue of the things

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.740
 that are to be retrieved.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.860
 And in my case when I built this map for the first time,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.340
 there was like 15,000 and it took me days to get this

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.920
 and so it would stop.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.940
 And so what I would do is I would read the first one

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.540
 into this geoload.py,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.440
 check to see if I already had it in my database.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.720
 If I didn't already have the database,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.040
 I would go into the API, pull the data down

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.520
 and I would put it in the database

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.080
 and then I would go to the next one.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:45.980
 The next one, the next one.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.100
 So you know I might get 1,000 in my database

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.940
 and then it blows up or I'm told I can't go any further.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.920
 So I wait 24 hours, I start it up

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.440
 and it reads the first 1,000 and says,

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.020
 oh they're all in the database already

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.800
 and then it starts at 1,000 and one.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.380
 And then it adds that and adds that

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.840
 and then until it stops and so it took me

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.760
 several days of processing to get this data right.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.740
 Now I didn't have a separate cleaning process

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.260
 because this data is pretty simple.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.780
 I was pulling out the JSON and latitude and longitude,

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.560
 et cetera and so I didn't have to do two separate processes

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.560
 to clean this data up.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.160
 It was clean enough right as I pulled it

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.080
 because I was talking to an API.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.200
 If you're talking to the HTML,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.340
 sometimes it gets nasty and ugly.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:31.240
 And so then I wrote this program that just reads through it.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.740
 It just does a select and you know reads through the stuff

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.480
 and it prints out some summary information

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.760
 and tells you what to do.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.520
 It also prints out and you'll see this pattern

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.280
 because I'm visualizing using browsers, HTML

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.600
 and this happens to be using the Google Maps API

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.200
 and putting all the data in a little JavaScript file.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.840
 So these end up being assignment statements in JavaScript.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.520
 You can take a look at that file.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.160
 And all the data shows up as assignment statements

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.360
 in the JavaScript and then when this HTML loads,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.900
 it reads this file and puts up all those pins

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.900
 as long as you have access to the in browser JavaScript API.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.120
 So the next thing we're going to talk about is PageRank

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.840
 which is spidering now HTML.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.240
 We talked a lot about this.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.080
 Spider HTML, get some links.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.560
 And so up next, we're going to actually build a real database,

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.680
 full featured search engine using PageRank.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.780
 (gentle music)

